<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Keate Award</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1871</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Keate Award was the 1871 arbitration by Robert Keate, Lieutenant-Governor of Natal, of rival claims to the land around the newly found diamond fields of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. It upheld the claims of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaas-waterboer">Nicolaas Waterboer</a> and of Tswana chiefs against the South African Republic. Waterboer then accepted British protection, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed the territory as Griqualand West later that year.</p></div>
